# Amusements: generators, simulators and diversions that are not quite
# games.  One stanza per program.  logisim (Maths & calculators) and
# textb (Screen toys) have lived here since the sheet was written and stay.

shot    areacode
cap     Looks up North American telephone area codes, as many as you give
cap     it, and answers each with the cities or region it covers.  The
cap     table is from the late 1980s, so codes added since are not in it,
cap     and a number it does not know is said to be no area code at all.
try     areacode 212 415 314 617 999
run     areacode 212 415 314 617 999
wait    4

shot    biory
size    44 100
cap     A biorhythm chart, in German.  It asks for a name (Name Vorname),
cap     a birth date as six digits, day month year (Geburtsdatum), and the
cap     first and last years to chart, two digits each (Gueltigkeitsbereich),
cap     writes the chart to Biory.Lis in the current directory and asks
cap     for the next name;
cap     RETURN there ends it.  Each month is three rows -- Koerper (body),
cap     Seele (emotion), Geist (mind) -- with K marking the day a cycle
cap     crosses from high to low or back.  It needs the Fortran run-time,
cap     os9lib, loaded first.
try     biory
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    2
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BIOA; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BIOA
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BIOA
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     biory
wait    5
send    Anna Bergmann\r
wait    3
send    140365\r
wait    3
send    88-88\r
wait    8
send    \r
wait    6
run     cat Biory.Lis | head -n 32
wait    5

shot    bio
size    52 80
cap     A biorhythm chart written in BASIC09, so it runs under runb.  It
cap     asks for a date and a birthday, both as day.month.year with the
cap     year in four digits (RETURN at the date prompt takes today), then
cap     whether you want that day's values
cap     (v) or a graph (g), and for a graph how many days.  Each row is a
cap     day; P, E and M are the physical, emotional and mental cycles,
cap     swinging from bad condition on the left to good on the right.
try     runb bio
run     load /h1/CMDS/runb
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     runb bio
wait    3
send    01.06.2025\r
wait    2
send    15.06.1970\r
wait    2
send    g\r
wait    3
send    30\r
wait    6

shot    england
size    40 100
cap     A year of weather for a tabletop game, one line a day: sky,
cap     temperature, precipitation, wind, snow on the ground and the day's
cap     notes, with the month's high, low and average at its foot.  This
cap     is the mid-Atlantic climate on the Gregorian calendar; `england 2'
cap     does two years.  The same program is built five more times for
cap     other climates and calendars: florida, georgia, minnesota, japan
cap     and shire.
try     england | head -n 37
os9     england ! head -n 37
run     england | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    florida
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its Gulf-coast profile: January is warm,
cap     and what falls is rain and the odd storm rather than snow.
try     florida | head -n 37
os9     florida ! head -n 37
run     florida | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    georgia
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its south-Atlantic profile: a mild, wet
cap     January, with a blizzard now and then when the temperature dips.
try     georgia | head -n 37
os9     georgia ! head -n 37
run     georgia | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    minnesota
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its north-Atlantic profile: January is
cap     cold throughout, and the snow depth in the seventh column grows
cap     with it.
try     minnesota | head -n 37
os9     minnesota ! head -n 37
run     minnesota | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    japan
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on its north-Pacific profile, with the months
cap     of the old Japanese calendar -- Rat, Bull and so on -- and its own
cap     notes: Shinto and Buddhist days, the full and new moons, the
cap     equinox.
try     japan | head -n 37
os9     japan ! head -n 37
run     japan | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    shire
size    40 100
cap     The weather program on the Shire calendar from the appendices of
cap     The Lord of the Rings -- Afteryule is the first month, Solmath the
cap     second -- over the mid-Atlantic climate.
try     shire | head -n 37
os9     shire ! head -n 37
run     shire | head -n 37
wait    10

shot    logisim
size    40 100
cap     A logic simulator.  It reads a circuit written as text -- one gate
cap     a line, named by type, with its input nodes and its output -- and
cap     runs it as a pulse diagram: a row for each node named on the
cap     circuit's `view' line, a column a step, # for high and _ for low,
cap     with the step numbers in the bars above and below.  This is the
cap     16-bit counter that ships with it in DOC/logisim, counting up from
cap     the top row; the other sample there is a master-slave flip-flop
cap     whose input you set at a prompt.  Esc stops the run.  PORT must
cap     name your terminal, because it reopens the keyboard through that
cap     path.
try     logisim DOC/logisim/counter.lsi
os9     setenv PORT /term; logisim DOC/logisim/counter.lsi
run     export PORT=/term
wait    1
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     logisim DOC/logisim/counter.lsi
wait    10
snap
send    \033
wait    2
send    y
wait    3

shot    name
cap     Invents names for the characters in a tabletop game, as many as
cap     you ask for, by dealing out vowels and consonants in turn with
cap     the letter frequencies of a Scrabble set; the apostrophe counts
cap     as a letter.
try     name 8
run     name 8
wait    4

shot    rndname
cap     The earlier version of `name': the same name-maker with every
cap     letter equally likely, so x, z and q turn up as often as t and n
cap     and the names come out more exotic.
try     rndname 6
run     rndname 6
wait    5

shot    newsgen
size    30 80
cap     A news bulletin made up at random from parts: a top story pieced
cap     together from public figures, deeds, places and reactions, then
cap     the weather.  Different every run.
try     newsgen
run     newsgen
wait    6

shot    pwgen
cap     Pronounceable passwords: give it a length, 4 to 16, and how many,
cap     and it strings syllables into words that can be said and so
cap     remembered.  It takes a while over each one.
try     pwgen 8 5
run     pwgen 8 5
wait    20

shot    rpoem
size    30 80
cap     Writes verses at random from a grammar and a word list; the
cap     number is how many, and without one it writes thirty.  Its
cap     grammar is in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     rpoem 3
run     rpoem 3
wait    8

shot    rstory
size    64 100
cap     A cumulative tale in the shape of The Old Woman and Her Pig: a
cap     child buys an animal that will not cross an obstacle and asks one
cap     helper after another, each refusing, until the chain runs back
cap     and everyone gets home.  The animal, the obstacle and every helper
cap     are drawn at random, so no two tellings match.  It writes the tale
cap     with formatting commands for tformat, which sets it justified on
cap     the page under a dated heading; on its own it prints them as
cap     `.b' and `.c' lines above the text.  Its grammar and word lists
cap     are in GAMES/SNOBOL.
try     rstory | tformat
os9     rstory ! tformat
run     rstory | tformat
wait    12

shot    scales
size    30 80
cap     Scales and chords for practice, dealt into a random order with a
cap     tick-box each and written to scales.lst in the current directory,
cap     or to a file you name.  The options say what goes in the deck:
cap     -d diatonic scales, -a altered scales, -m modes, -c chords.  Each
cap     entry gives the key signature and the spelling.
try     scales -d -c
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/SCLA > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SCLA > /nil 2>/nil
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/SCLA
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     scales -d -c
wait    6
run     cat scales.lst | head -n 16
wait    4

shot    touchtype
cap     A typing game.  Words fall down the screen and each has to be
cap     typed, followed by a space or RETURN, before it reaches the
cap     bottom; the top line counts hits and misses.  It first asks
cap     whether you want instructions (y or n) and a level, 1 to 3.  This
cap     is level 2 a few seconds in; Esc ends the game and scores it in
cap     words per minute.
try     touchtype
run     touchtype
wait    3
send    n
wait    2
send    2
wait    6
keys    the
wait    2
snap
wait    1
send    \033
wait    3

shot    travesty
size    30 80
cap     Rewrites a text as plausible nonsense.  It learns which letters
cap     follow which in the input -- runs of five, unless -o says
cap     otherwise -- and writes new text with the same habits, as many
cap     characters as -n asks for.  Here it has read the Star Trek
cap     quotations in GAMES/COOKIE and invents its own, attributions and
cap     stardates included.
try     travesty -n600 < GAMES/COOKIE/startrek
os9     travesty -n600 <GAMES/COOKIE/startrek
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     travesty -n600 < GAMES/COOKIE/startrek
wait    12

shot    textb
size    32 80
cap     The Mandelbrot set drawn in ASCII, the number of iterations at
cap     each point turned into a character.  It asks for the lower-left
cap     corner, the width of the square to draw and the iteration limit;
cap     -2.3, -2.0, 4.0 and 32 gives the whole set, cardioid and bulb
cap     both, and a smaller range zooms in.
try     textb
run     textb
wait    4
send    -2.3\r
wait    3
send    -2.0\r
wait    3
send    4.0\r
wait    3
send    32\r
wait    60

shot    phoon
size    25 80
cap     phoon draws the phase of the moon, lit from the correct side, with
cap     the days and hours to the next quarter beside it.  With no argument
cap     it draws tonight's; give a date -- year month day -- for any other,
cap     and `-l' sets the height.  Once in a while the moon turns out to be
cap     made of green cheese.
try     phoon 2025 10 7
run     phoon 2025 10 7
wait    3

shot    globe
size    24 80
cap     globe draws the Earth as it is lit right now, one of thirty ASCII
cap     views spread across the day, so the globe turns from one run to the
cap     next as the hours pass.  It takes no options.
try     globe
run     globe
wait    3

shot    spew
size    24 80
cap     spew builds mock National Enquirer headlines from a grammar of
cap     phrases and slots -- almost a yacc in reverse.  `spew' makes one,
cap     `spew 5' makes five; point it at your own rules file for other
cap     kinds of nonsense.
try     spew 4
run     spew 4
wait    2

shot    chef
size    24 80
cap     chef rewrites English into the Swedish Chef's mock accent a letter
cap     and syllable at a time -- the becomes zee, w becomes v, o becomes
cap     oo -- and barks "Bork Bork Bork!" at the end of every sentence.
cap     Pipe any text through it.
try     echo "The Swedish chef boils the water and stirs the soup." | chef
run     echo "The Swedish chef boils the water and stirs the soup." | chef
wait    2

shot    fudd
size    24 80
cap     fudd rewrites text the way Elmer Fudd would say it -- r and l both
cap     become w, th becomes d -- so any sentence comes out in his hunting
cap     lisp.  Pipe text through it.
try     echo "Be very very quiet, I am hunting rabbits." | fudd
run     echo "Be very very quiet, I am hunting rabbits." | fudd
wait    2

shot    drawl
size    24 80
cap     drawl gives text a broad Texan accent, dropping g's and stretching
cap     vowels -- going becomes goin', to becomes tuh, the becomes thuh.
cap     Pipe any text through it.
try     echo "I am going to the store to buy some food." | drawl
run     echo "I am going to the store to buy some food." | drawl
wait    2

shot    lame
size    24 80
cap     lame rewrites its arguments into IRC "leet-speak" -- o becomes 0 or
cap     (), you becomes U, and becomes &, i becomes 1, e becomes 3 -- with a
cap     little randomness, the way a lamer types.  Give it a sentence.
try     lame you are so cool and elite
run     lame you are so cool and elite
wait    2

shot    trigraph
size    24 80
cap     A program that prints its own source, spelled with ANSI C trigraphs:
cap     ??( and ??) for brackets, ??< and ??> for braces, ??= for #, ??/ for \.
try     trigraph | head -n 22
os9     trigraph ! head -n 22
run     clear
wait    1
run     trigraph | head -n 22
wait    3
